==== Wave 2: Mechanics

===== Turboprop Power

+ *Torque* — the power gauge: turbine engines talk in torque, not rpm. Climb and cruise power are set by torque targets; know your limits for the phase of flight.
+ *ITT / temperature limits* — turbine inlet temperature is the one gauge you must never exceed for long: hot days, high power, and slow climb rates push it. Watch it like the stall warning of Wave 0.
+ *Propeller management* — props stay at their governing rpm in normal flight; the interesting parts are at the edges: *beta* (pitch control for ground handling) and *reverse* (braking on landing), plus feathering if an engine stops.
+ *Engine-out reality* — a turboprop with one engine feathered still flies; the asymmetric thrust demands rudder discipline, and the checklist demands practice. Run the drill at altitude with a simulated failure.

===== Pressurization & Cabin

+ *The system* — bleed air from the engines keeps the cabin at a comfortable altitude while the aircraft climbs high. Set the cabin altitude target and the controller climbs/descends the cabin gently.
+ *The climb/descent rule* — climb at the aircraft's cabin-rate limit, not just the flight-path one: the passengers (and the dog) feel the cabin rate long before the aircraft rate.
+ *Spoilers/dumps* — learn the aircraft's descent and cabin-pressure dump controls; a fast, controlled descent is a turbine skill.

===== FMS & Navigation

+ *The FMS* — the route computer: load the plan (exported from LittleNavMap), add the SID/STAR and the approach, and let the box fly lateral and vertical guidance.
+ *RNAV/ILS approaches* — from the RNAV/GPS approach with its vertical guidance to the raw ILS localizer/glideslope: know both, fly both, brief both from the plate in AviTab.
+ *Holds & missed approaches* — the racetrack and the escape: programmed in the FMS, flown by hand when it is not. Practice them; the airline wave assumes you can.

===== IFR Discipline

+ *The instrument scan* — attitude, airspeed, altitude, heading, vertical speed, cross-checked against the approach plate's profile. In cloud, the scan is your life.
+ *Minimums* — every approach has decision altitudes/heights and visibility minimums; below minimums, the go-around is not an option, it is the procedure.
+ *Radio work* — readbacks, position reports, and the quiet confidence of someone who files and flies the system: the sim's ATC plus the radio basics from Wave 0, at IFR pace.
+ *De-icing* — boots, heat, and the rule that ice is a *leave* condition, not a *push-through* condition: plan the escape before you enter.

===== Workload Management

+ *Aviate, navigate, communicate* — in that order. When the workload spikes, the airplane and the path come first; the radio waits.
+ *The checklist flow* — flows and checklists: do the flow from memory, confirm with the list. Wave 2 is where this habit becomes permanent.
+ *Single-pilot triage* — delegate to the systems that earn it (autopilot, FMS) and keep the decisions that are yours (weather, fuel, minimums, go-around).
